      en: 'TypeScript is JavaScript with a static type system checked at compile time; the types are then erased and what runs is ordinary JavaScript.\n\nWhat it gives you:\n1. Errors caught before running: a typo in a property name, passing the wrong type, forgetting to handle undefined.\n2. Living documentation: a signature states the contract without comments that go stale.\n3. Safe refactoring: renaming a field immediately reveals every affected call site.\n4. A much better editor experience with accurate autocompletion.\n\nAn essential point to state: types disappear at runtime. TypeScript does not protect you from bad data arriving from an API or a user form — you still need real runtime validation (Zod, for example) at the system boundary.\n\nThe honest cost: an extra build step, a learning curve, and occasionally gnarly library types. But on any project past a certain size, or with more than one developer, the benefit clearly outweighs it.',
