      en: 'An annotation states a type explicitly; inference is the compiler working it out for you.\n\n```ts\nlet name: string = "Sara";     // explicit\nlet name = "Sara";              // inferred: string\n\nfunction add(a: number, b: number): number {\n  return a + b;\n}\n\nconst user = { id: 1, name: "Sara" };   // { id: number; name: string }\n```\n\nWhen do I annotate and when do I let inference work?\n• Always annotate function parameters — the compiler cannot infer them.\n• Let inference handle variables initialised with an obvious value; writing `const count: number = 5` is noise.\n• Annotate return types on public library APIs and complex functions, because the annotation acts as a contract and prevents accidental changes.\n\nAn important difference between let and const:\n```ts\nlet status = "active";        // string\nconst status = "active";      // "active" — a literal type\n```\nBecause const cannot be reassigned, the compiler infers the narrower literal type — which is the basis for how literal unions and narrowing work later.',
