      en: '```ts\nlet s: string;\nlet n: number;\nlet b: boolean;\nlet big: bigint;\nlet sym: symbol;\nlet u: undefined;\nlet nl: null;\n\nlet arr: number[];              // or Array<number>\nlet tup: [string, number];      // a tuple of fixed length and types\nlet anything: any;              // disables checking — avoid\nlet safe: unknown;              // safe: must be narrowed before use\nfunction fail(): never {}       // never returns\nfunction log(): void {}         // returns no useful value\n```\n\nThree types deserve careful distinction:\n\n• any switches off type checking entirely for every operation on the value. Using it defeats the purpose of TypeScript and spreads to everything it touches.\n• unknown is the safe version of any. Anything can be assigned to it, but you cannot use it until you narrow it:\n```ts\nfunction handle(value: unknown) {\n  if (typeof value === "string") value.toUpperCase();   // safe\n}\n```\n• never is a type with no possible value. A function that always throws returns never, and its most valuable use is exhaustiveness checking in a switch.\n\nA practical rule: when handling external data use unknown rather than any — it forces you to validate.',
