      en: 'A union means a value may be one of several types; a literal type restricts it to one exact value.\n\n```ts\ntype ID = string | number;                          // union\ntype Status = "idle" | "loading" | "success" | "error";  // literal union\n\nfunction setStatus(status: Status) {}\nsetStatus("loading");     // fine\nsetStatus("Loading");     // compile error\n```\n\nA literal union is usually better than an enum or a bare string: lighter, visible in autocompletion, and it emits no runtime code.\n\nTo work with a union you must narrow it first:\n```ts\nfunction format(value: string | number) {\n  if (typeof value === "string") return value.trim();   // string here\n  return value.toFixed(2);                              // number here\n}\n```\n\nAn important point: on a union you can only access what every member has in common — deliberately, because it prevents unfounded assumptions.\n\nThe counterpart is the intersection &, which requires all properties together:\n```ts\ntype Admin = User & { permissions: string[] };\n```\n\nOne last note: use `as const` to turn an array or object into literal types:\n```ts\nconst ROLES = ["admin", "user"] as const;\ntype Role = typeof ROLES[number];    // "admin" | "user"\n```',
